1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:05,100
As you get more users, your database
grows and your tables get bigger.

2
00:00:05,470 --> 00:00:08,280
Now it might take a little bit longer
for your app to load and you're

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:09,790
now having performance issues.

4
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:13,090
One thing that you can always use
to fix this is a database index.

5
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:15,240
In this video, we'll go over what your

6
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:18,860
database does when there is no
index, what an index actually is,

7
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:21,790
how it helps find things so
fast and helps performance,

8
00:00:22,090 --> 00:00:25,830
and the different types of indexes,
the mistakes that people make when

9
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:27,340
they use database indexes.

10
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,430
To understand why a
database index matters,

11
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:32,480
especially for performance,
you first have to understand

12
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:34,870
what your database does without an index.

13
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:38,850
Say we have a bookstore, and the
books table in our database has a

14
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,980
million rows, we have a
million books in there,

15
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:43,280
and we run this query.

16
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,540
Without an index, the database has
no idea where author 42's books

17
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:49,630
are, so it does the only thing it can do.

18
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:52,740
It starts at the first row and
it checks every single row,

19
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:57,020
all one million of them, and that
is called a full table scan.

20
00:00:57,230 --> 00:01:00,040
And in many cases, it
can be very problematic.

21
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:03,570
When your table has maybe 10,
100 rows, maybe it wouldn't be a

22
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:05,400
problem, maybe you wouldn't notice,

23
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:08,280
but at a million rows,
then it's a problem.

24
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:11,980
What is a database index and how
does it solve this problem of speed

25
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,000
and performance?

26
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,690
The easiest way to think about it
is the index at the back of a book.

27
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:19,150
If you want to find every page
that mentions a particular word,

28
00:01:19,170 --> 00:01:20,960
you don't read the whole
book from the beginning.

29
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:23,690
You go to the index at the back,
it's sorted alphabetically,

30
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:26,420
you find the word, and it
tells you the page number.

31
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:28,720
A database index works the same way.

32
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:32,340
It's a separate structure that
stores the values from a column in

33
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:36,670
sorted order, and for each value, it
keeps a pointer to where that row

34
00:01:36,690 --> 00:01:37,500
actually lives.

35
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:39,550
And it only stores the
column and the pointer,

36
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:42,910
so it's way much more smaller
than the table itself.

37
00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:46,770
Now what mechanism does a database
index use to find things so fast?

38
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:50,180
I read an article on this, and I love
the way the writer explained it,

39
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:51,260
and I'm going to use the example.

40
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,420
I will link the article in
the description box as well.

41
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:55,530
Say you have an index on the email column,

42
00:01:55,550 --> 00:01:57,220
and you're looking for bob@email.com.

43
00:01:57,650 --> 00:02:00,150
The database opens the
index in the middle,

44
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:01,370
and maybe sees dave@email.com.

45
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:05,740
Bob comes before dave,
so it moves to the left.

46
00:02:05,970 --> 00:02:08,470
Now it sees alice@email.com.

47
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:10,980
Bob comes after Alice, so
it moves to the right,

48
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:14,830
and it keeps moving strategically like
this until it finds bob@email.com

49
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:19,390
in the index. Bob's entry in the
index has a pointer to where Bob's

50
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:20,830
row is in the table.

51
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:22,040
So with this strategy,

52
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:26,470
even with a million rows, you can
get to the row you're looking for in

53
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:28,130
about 20 steps or so.

54
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:32,040
Without an index, you will do up to
a million checks in the worst case

55
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:36,313
scenario. Most databases store their
indexes in a structure called a

56
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:40,520
B-tree, and a B-tree works the way
that the example from the article

57
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:44,340
showed. So even with a
table with millions of rows,

58
00:02:44,470 --> 00:02:48,550
you usually only need a few
levels to get to the right place.

59
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:52,156
Adding a database index will
literally up your query optimisation

60
00:02:52,176 --> 00:02:54,200
game, and it's kind of a one-liner.

61
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:56,040
In SQL, it looks something like this.

62
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:58,890
If you are using Laravel, for
example, you simply add it in your

63
00:02:58,910 --> 00:03:00,160
migration like this.

64
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:03,480
Now when you search books by author,
the database goes to the index

65
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,770
first, finds author 42, and
goes straight to those rows.

66
00:03:06,790 --> 00:03:10,170
Once you've added your database
index, you can confirm whether your

67
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:11,670
query is actually using it or not.

68
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:12,820
You can ask the database.

69
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:15,290
Just put the word EXPLAIN
in front of your query.

70
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:18,240
In PostgreSQL, you will see "Seq Scan".

71
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:21,740
It means that the database
is reading every single row,

72
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:25,300
and if you see "Index Scan", it
means that the database is using your

73
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:28,200
index. "Seq Scan", "sequential scan".

74
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:31,550
In MySQL, the results
will come back in a small

75
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:34,110
table, so there are
two columns to look at.

76
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:37,180
If it says "ALL", that means
it's doing a full table scan.

77
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:38,820
If it says something
like "ref" or "range",

78
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:39,940
then it's using an index.

79
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:42,910
So if your query is slow, and you
first want to confirm what is going

80
00:03:42,930 --> 00:03:45,020
on, you can use, and that confirms to you,

81
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,950
is this query doing a full
scan, or is it using an index?

82
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:49,930
There are a few types of database index.

83
00:03:49,950 --> 00:03:52,690
You hear people talk about
B-tree, hash, bitmap,

84
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:54,400
and many, many like that.

85
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:57,710
I'm just only going to focus
on three in this video.

86
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:59,580
The first one is B-tree.

87
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:03,820
B-tree. The B-tree is the default
one, so when you create an index

88
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:06,730
without saying what type it is,
this is the one that you get.

89
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:07,900
So it's very common.

90
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:11,420
It can find exact matches,
like the user with this email.

91
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:15,200
It can find ranges, like books
priced between 10 and 15.

92
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:17,780
It can sort, like showing
the newest order first,

93
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:19,920
and it can find values by how they start,

94
00:04:20,010 --> 00:04:21,960
like every book title
that starts with "Harry".

95
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:24,000
And that works because
the index is sorted,

96
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:27,160
so all the titles that start with
"Harry" sit next to each other.

97
00:04:27,210 --> 00:04:29,890
The second commonly used type of database

98
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:31,390
index is the unique index.

99
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:33,660
It makes sure that no two
rows have the same value.

100
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:36,750
For example, no two user can
sign up with the same email.

101
00:04:36,770 --> 00:04:39,280
Your primary key already has one.

102
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:41,700
And the third one, this one
kind of always confuses me.

103
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:45,440
The third one is the composite index,
which is an index on more than

104
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:49,690
one column. So say you index last
name and first name together in that

105
00:04:49,710 --> 00:04:51,960
order. It helps you when
you search by last name,

106
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:54,590
and it helps you when you search
by last name and first name.

107
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:57,230
But when you search by only first
name, it usually doesn't help.

108
00:04:57,250 --> 00:04:59,870
A little bit of a reason, let's
use the example with phone books to

109
00:04:59,890 --> 00:05:02,550
understand it. Think of a
phone book sorted by surname.

110
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:07,330
All the Adeyemis are together,
and all the Okafors are together.

111
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:11,130
And inside each surname, it
is sorted by first name.

112
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:15,160
So in the Okafors, Ada Okafor
will come before Chidi Okafor.

113
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:17,570
So if I'm looking for
Ada Okafor, it's easy.

114
00:05:17,590 --> 00:05:20,710
I go to the O's, I find
Okafor, and then I find Ada.

115
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:23,380
But what if I want everyone
whose first name is Ada?

116
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:25,756
There's probably Ada Adeyemi in the front,

117
00:05:25,776 --> 00:05:29,720
and Ada Okafor in the middle,
and Ada Williams near the end.

118
00:05:29,770 --> 00:05:33,120
They are spread across the whole
phone book because the book is sorted

119
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:34,310
by surname first.

120
00:05:34,330 --> 00:05:37,570
So nothing groups the Adas together,
and I would have to read every

121
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:38,420
page to find them.

122
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:43,870
So that's exactly what happens with
the composite index on last name, first

123
00:05:44,530 --> 00:05:49,940
name. So the tip is to put the column
you search by most often first.

124
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:52,930
And for the rest, there are
different kinds of searches.

125
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:55,820
For the rest of the database index types,

126
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:59,638
we have the hash index, which
is for exact matches only,

127
00:05:59,658 --> 00:06:04,480
you know, like looking up someone's
login session by its exact token,

128
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:06,420
it cannot do range or sorting.

129
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:08,210
So most people just use B-tree.

130
00:06:08,370 --> 00:06:10,960
A bitmap is for columns
with only a few values,

131
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:14,220
like a status column, that's only
active or inactive or banned.

132
00:06:14,290 --> 00:06:16,930
You mostly see it in databases
that are built for reports,

133
00:06:17,310 --> 00:06:20,241
yeah. And then we have spatial index,
that one is mostly for location

134
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:22,231
data, like when you
search a restaurant name,

135
00:06:22,251 --> 00:06:26,330
a coffee shop near me, or when a
ride app looks for drivers close to

136
00:06:26,350 --> 00:06:31,040
you. So it's groups, places that
are near each other on the map,

137
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:33,480
so the app only checks places around you,

138
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:36,740
instead of every coffee shop or
every restaurant in the world.

139
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:38,650
And then we have the
full-text index as well.

140
00:06:38,670 --> 00:06:40,380
It is for searching inside text.

141
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:44,000
For example, when you search an
online store for wireless headphones,

142
00:06:44,150 --> 00:06:47,000
it finds every product description
with those words in it,

143
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:50,502
right? So it works by indexing each
word in the text instead of the

144
00:06:50,522 --> 00:06:54,043
whole value. And there's also
covering index and partial index.

145
00:06:54,063 --> 00:06:58,160
You also hear about clustered
and non-clustered indexes.

146
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:00,840
And the difference between
those is very simple.

147
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:04,708
The clustered index decides the
order the table's rows are actually

148
00:07:04,728 --> 00:07:06,690
stored. Think of this
as a phone book again,

149
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:08,216
the data itself is sorted by surname.

150
00:07:08,236 --> 00:07:10,306
So once you find the
name, you find everything.

151
00:07:10,326 --> 00:07:13,638
A table can only have one clustered
index because the rows can only be

152
00:07:13,658 --> 00:07:14,840
stored in one order.

153
00:07:14,870 --> 00:07:17,300
A non-clustered index
is a separate structure

154
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:18,990
that points back to the row.

155
00:07:19,010 --> 00:07:21,220
Think of that as the index
at the back of the book,

156
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:23,280
it tells you where to go and go there.

157
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:26,130
So a table can have lots
of non-clustered indexes.

158
00:07:26,540 --> 00:07:30,330
So this is the difference between
clustered and non-clustered indexes.

159
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:34,580
So now that begs the question, if
database indexes makes queries so

160
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:38,830
fast, why don't we just index
every column and be done with it?

161
00:07:38,850 --> 00:07:41,371
We cannot, because every index has a cost.

162
00:07:41,391 --> 00:07:43,840
Every time you insert,
update and delete a row,

163
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:47,320
the database also has to update
every index on that table.

164
00:07:47,370 --> 00:07:50,720
So if your orders table has six
indexes, every new order means

165
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:52,860
updating the whole table
and all six indexes.

166
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:56,950
So the more database indexes you
have, the slower your writes get.

167
00:07:56,970 --> 00:07:59,680
And also indexes also
take up storage space.

168
00:07:59,700 --> 00:08:03,850
And for a small table, the database
will often skip your index and

169
00:08:03,870 --> 00:08:07,090
just scan the table anyway, because
that's faster for a small table,

170
00:08:07,110 --> 00:08:09,860
you have just like 10 rows, it's
faster to read them than to go

171
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:11,430
through an index first.

172
00:08:11,450 --> 00:08:15,660
So what you do is that you only
index the columns that you actually

173
00:08:15,710 --> 00:08:17,960
search on or filter on or join or sort by,

174
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:19,380
you don't index everything.

175
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:22,340
But because as good as it is,
it can also get costly too.

176
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:25,050
And there are several mistakes
you can make that can stop your

177
00:08:25,070 --> 00:08:26,190
database index from

178
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:27,230
working as well.

179
00:08:27,250 --> 00:08:31,350
You can have a database index and
the database still won't use it.

180
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:33,910
And these are the common
mistakes that cause it.

181
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:36,180
The first one is wrapping
your column in a function.

182
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:40,550
For example, in this case, a normal
index on email is sorted by the

183
00:08:40,570 --> 00:08:42,860
emails exactly as they were stored.

184
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,880
The lowercase versions
are not in that index.

185
00:08:46,210 --> 00:08:50,220
So the database can't use it here,
unless you create an index on the

186
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,240
same expression.

187
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:56,224
The second is you can also be
adding a wildcard at the start of a

188
00:08:56,244 --> 00:08:59,350
LIKE. The index is sorted
by how each value starts.

189
00:08:59,370 --> 00:09:03,010
And if you don't know how it starts,
the index cannot help you find

190
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:07,010
it. Another database index
gotcha is that in MySQL,

191
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:10,940
when you add a foreign key, the
database creates an index on that

192
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:14,150
column for you automatically,
if there isn't one already.

193
00:09:14,210 --> 00:09:16,180
But in PostgreSQL, it doesn't.

194
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:19,480
So if you're on Postgres, or you've
moved from MySQL to Postgres,

195
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:23,160
you have to check and confirm that
your foreign key columns actually

196
00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:27,038
have indexes. And it's one of the
common reasons why joins suddenly

197
00:09:27,058 --> 00:09:30,810
get slow. So again, to reiterate
when to actually use a database

198
00:09:30,830 --> 00:09:32,220
index, use it when

199
00:09:32,910 --> 00:09:35,898
a column keeps showing up
in your WHERE clauses,

200
00:09:35,918 --> 00:09:37,700
your joins or your ORDER BY.

201
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:40,500
So say you are sorting a
million orders by date,

202
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:42,300
and you only show the first 10.

203
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:46,550
If there's no index, the database
has to look at a million rows and

204
00:09:46,570 --> 00:09:49,880
sort them before it can give you those 10.

205
00:09:50,390 --> 00:09:53,382
But with an index, it reads them
in order and stops after the first

206
00:09:53,402 --> 00:09:57,570
10. Also, when you use EXPLAIN, and
you realize that a query is doing

207
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:02,740
a full table scan, and it is slow,
then maybe you need an index there.

208
00:10:02,910 --> 00:10:05,770
So if your query is slow,
you probably should consider

209
00:10:06,210 --> 00:10:07,820
adding an index there.

210
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:09,470
And if it's doing a full scan.

211
00:10:09,490 --> 00:10:14,500
Also, when a table gets more read,
like you read it a lot more than

212
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:17,160
you write it, that's also a good
situation for a database index.

213
00:10:17,180 --> 00:10:19,310
But you shouldn't do that
when the table is small.

214
00:10:19,330 --> 00:10:22,270
Or when the table is constantly
written to and rarely searched,

215
00:10:22,290 --> 00:10:25,110
then you probably don't need a
database index in that situation.

216
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:27,810
So that's it with database indexes. Bye.
